#### what this step proves: THE ROUND'S THEME. Under collector overload the twin under-reports
#### a perfectly healthy flow by 2.76x while every endpoint answers 200/success in <5 ms.
2026-09-03T03:36:19+08:00

Design: three cells, the SAME real traffic in all three (iperf3 UDP 20 Mbit/s, 1400 B, h1->h2,
55 s), flow count held at 1. The ONLY thing that changes is the load offered to the collector's
UDP socket. A and C are the controls, taken 1 and 2 minutes either side of B on the same machine
with the identical command.

  cell                 wire ground truth (iperf3 sender)   sFlow flood
  A_control_before     131 MBytes  20.0 Mbit/s  0/98213    none
  B_flood              131 MBytes  20.0 Mbit/s  0/98213    17 330 000 datagrams in 32.0 s = 541 547/s
  C_control_after      131 MBytes  20.0 Mbit/s  0/98213    none

0% loss on the wire in all three -- byte-identical iperf3 lines. The flow really was there.

WHAT THE TWIN PUBLISHED for that flow (median of 30 one-second polls of
/ndt/get_detected_flow_data, field estimated_flow_sending_rate_bps_in_the_proceeding_1sec_timeslot):

  A_control_before   19 688 106 bit/s     -1.6% vs the 20 000 000 offered
  B_flood             7 136 938 bit/s    -64.3%   <-- 2.76x under-report
  C_control_after    20 672 512 bit/s     +3.4%

AND WHILE THAT WAS TRUE:
  * /ndt/get_average_link_usage           HTTP 200 "status":"success" in 0.00056 s
  * /ndt/get_detected_flow_data           HTTP 200                    in 0.00038 s
  * /ndt/get_graph_data                   HTTP 200                    in 0.00417 s
  * /ndt/get_openflow_capacity            HTTP 200                    in 0.00079 s
    -- every one of them faster under the flood than in the control cell.
  * the flow stayed "active" 30/30 polls in all three windows in ALL THREE cells, so a consumer
    watching liveness saw nothing move.
  * avg_link_usage went UP, not down: 0.0103 (A) -> 0.0221 (B) -> 0.0133 (C). The flood's
    fabricated samples were credited to a real edge (agent 192.168.123.11, port 1->2), so the
    published utilisation for that edge roughly DOUBLED while the edge carried nothing extra.
  * the only trace anywhere is 33 WARN lines in .test_run/logs/kernel.log (0 in cell A after the
    residue of the previous step, 33 in B, 0 in C) -- and by step 08 no /ndt/ endpoint carries
    that fact.

WHY IT IS LOSS AND NOT CPU STARVATION OF THE DRAIN LOOP:
  the known per-flow bias (COMMON-BRIEF section 11) has no time denominator, so its error grows
  WITH the loop period: a CPU-starved kernel would over-report, not under-report. The number fell
  by 64%. The surviving-sample fraction predicted by step 09 at this offered rate is ~0.28-0.36;
  the observed 0.357 of the true rate sits inside it. Loss explains it; starvation works against it.
  (A and C reading 19.7 M and 20.7 M against a true 20.0 M is that same optimistic bias, small at
  one flow. It makes B's under-report a LOWER bound.)

